That's your point not Rast's point.  The two of you disagree it
appears.

My take is different - When damaging monsters start becoming
common, that's when I want my outer armor to be immune to that
type of damage.  If I have the right type of armor items I might
want to proof them or enchant them depending on what type of
damage I expect to encounter next.  I also tend to carry some
rustproof weapon for rust monsters - elven daggers and elven
broadsword with leather or dex gloves, leather or speed boots and
some sort of cloak work great against rust monsters and black
puddings but suck against brown puddings.

At some point I'll have proofed armor and that context no longer
matters.  Until I do, I consider which monster I am most likely
to face, what type of damage it will do to my current weapons
and armor, then decide proof or enchant.  Early on enchant tends
to win.  Midgame proof tends to win.  Late game whatever it takes
to max out one specific item tends to win.  A luxury game includes
a fireproof teeshirt and rustproof pick axe ...



